From 699c74f41c7bed1494a4e50c6778c15f7fb4b32b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:53:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Live Tracking: base long-journey detection on the static
 schedule, not live data

Users reported the RailRadar-preferred long-journey behavior working
on the first connect but flipping back to RailKit-only on a
reconnect for the exact same train/date. Root cause: is_long_journey
was computed from timeline_json, rebuilt from RailKit's LIVE
trackTrain response every poll - RailKit's well-documented live-state
flakiness (this module's whole history of long-journey-only bugs)
could make that duration calc fail on one poll and succeed on the
next, for the same real journey.

Added gps_tracking.estimate_journey_duration_minutes_from_route,
which reads the same day+time fields off the STATIC schedule
(train_info_data/getTrainInfo's route, parsed via parse_route - now
captures each stop's `day` field too) instead of the live timeline.
A train's published schedule can't change poll to poll, so this
gives the same answer on the first connect and the hundredth
reconnect. app.py now prefers this and only falls back to the old
live-timeline calc on the rare poll where train_info_data itself
couldn't be fetched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MGKaCWBEc2rtTTsPnQDkYV
